/** The two articles carried over from the old blog. */
function veahealth_content_posts() {
	return array(
		array(
			'slug' => 'are-cosmetic-dentistry-operations-age-restricted',
			'title' => 'Are cosmetic dentistry operations age restricted?',
			'excerpt' => 'Where age genuinely matters in cosmetic dentistry, where it does not, and what a clinic should check before treating a younger or older patient.',
			'date' => '2026-02-04',
			'cover' => 'blog-cover-dental-ceramic-sand',
			'alt' => 'Ceramic tooth form resting on rippled sand, lit by a single soft light',
			'content' => <<<'VH_POST_0'
<p>Taking care of your oral health is a lifelong commitment that requires consistency, awareness, and the right dental practices. Whether you’re maintaining your daily hygiene routine or visiting the dentist for regular checkups, understanding your dental needs is essential for achieving a bright, healthy smile. In this blog post, we’ll explore key aspects of dental care, including oral hygiene habits, nutrition, regular checkups, preventive care, and more.</p>
<p>Good oral hygiene is the foundation of healthy teeth and gums. Brushing twice a day and flossing regularly remove plaque buildup that can lead to cavities and gum disease. It’s equally important to use fluoride toothpaste and replace your toothbrush every three months. Cleaning your tongue and rinsing with an antibacterial mouthwash can further reduce bacteria and keep your breath fresh. By maintaining these habits daily, you can prevent common dental issues and enjoy long-term oral health.</p>
<p>Scheduling regular dental visits is vital for maintaining strong and healthy teeth. Dentists can detect early signs of decay, gum problems, or oral infections that may not be visible at home. Professional cleanings help remove tartar and plaque that regular brushing can’t. Whether it’s every six months or as recommended by your dentist, these checkups ensure early prevention and long-lasting oral care. Consistent visits also help you build confidence in your smile and maintain good dental hygiene routines.</p>
<p>Your diet plays a major role in your dental health. Foods rich in calcium, phosphorus, and vitamins help strengthen enamel and gums. Include more fruits, vegetables, dairy, and water in your diet while avoiding sugary snacks and carbonated drinks that erode enamel. Limit acidic foods like citrus fruits or sodas, and if you consume them, rinse your mouth afterward. Remember, proper nutrition doesn’t just benefit your body — it directly affects your smile and overall oral wellness.</p>
<p>Rest and relaxation also contribute to good dental health. Stress can lead to teeth grinding, jaw pain, or even gum inflammation. Getting enough sleep and managing stress helps your body repair tissues, including those in your mouth. If you notice symptoms like jaw soreness or tooth sensitivity upon waking, consult your dentist — you may need a night guard or stress management plan. A balanced lifestyle supports not only your body but your oral health too.</p>
<p>Maintaining good dental manners and habits ensures long-term oral wellness. Avoid using your teeth as tools to open packages, chewing hard objects like ice or pens, or grinding your teeth unconsciously. Encourage children to develop healthy brushing habits early, turning it into a fun daily routine. Practicing mindful oral habits helps you protect your teeth from unnecessary damage and promotes confidence in your smile wherever you go.</p>
<p>Preventive dental care is the key to avoiding costly and painful treatments later. Regular scaling, polishing, and fluoride treatments help keep your mouth clean and protected. Don’t skip your dental X-rays when recommended — they help identify hidden cavities or bone issues early. Routine dental hygiene also includes cleaning your gums gently and maintaining hydration for saliva production. Consistent preventive care keeps your teeth strong, your gums healthy, and your smile radiant.</p>
<p>Committing to a proper dental care routine, maintaining a nutritious diet, and visiting your dentist regularly will ensure you enjoy a confident, healthy smile for years to come. Your oral health is a reflection of your overall well-being — treat it with care and attention. With the right habits and regular checkups, you can keep your teeth strong, your gums healthy, and your smile shining bright every day!</p>
VH_POST_0,
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'smile-makeover-transforming-confidence-through-dentistry',
			'title' => 'Smile makeover: transforming confidence through dentistry',
			'excerpt' => 'What a smile makeover actually involves, which treatments it combines, and how to judge whether the result will look like you.',
			'date' => '2026-02-04',
			'cover' => 'journey-travel-flatlay',
			'alt' => 'Calm flat-lay of travel items on linen in warm morning light',
